          "dataType": "value",
          "angle": 270
        },
        {
          "id": "right",
          "label": "right",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "value",
          "angle": 0
        },
        {
          "id": "bottom",
          "label": "bottom",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "value",
          "angle": 90
        },
        {
          "id": "left",
          "label": "left",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "value",
          "angle": 180
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "graph-defined-handles"
        },
        "markdown": "# Proposed Handle Definition and Resolution Model\n\nA Handle definition is a normal graph artifact with one narrow exposed **handle surface**. Its similarity to Glyph ends at the fact that both may be connected structurally to a Declaration. Handle has its own resolution rule because Ports may override it.\n\n```text\nHandle Definition Graph\n\nHandle Declaration\n  ├─ default-view / summary / icon / glyph   ordinary graph participation\n  ├─ landing-surface                         ordinary graph entry\n  └─ handle-surface ──> Handle View ──> SVG, HTML, or Canvas\n\nHandle surface contract\n  • canonical viewBox / intrinsic aspect ratio\n  • normalized anchor or hotspot\n  • canonical zero-angle direction\n  • visual states: idle, hover, active, connected, invalid\n\nNode Definition Graph\n\nNode Declaration\n  └─ default-handle ──> Handle Definition.handle-surface\n\nPort A\n  └─ handle (unconnected) ──> inherits Declaration default\n\nPort B\n  └─ handle ────────────────> Alternate Handle Definition.handle-surface\n                                      overrides Declaration default\n\nPort C\n  └─ handle (unconnected) ──> inherits Declaration default\n```\n\n## Resolution rule\n\nFor a visible endpoint:\n\n1. Resolve the endpoint's Port node.\n2. If its structural `handle` port is connected, use that Handle definition.\n3. Otherwise resolve the owning node Declaration's `default-handle` relationship.\n4. If neither exists, use the system fallback Handle.\n5. Apply use-site size and rotation after resolving the Handle definition.\n\n`Port.handle ?? Declaration.default-handle ?? system-handle`\n\n## Ownership boundary\n\n- **Handle definition graph:** SVG/HTML/Canvas content, intrinsic geometry, hotspot, canonical orientation, and visual states.\n- **Declaration default-handle reference:** normal presentation for the node's endpoints.\n- **Port handle reference:** presentation override for that one semantic endpoint.\n- **Port:** identity, direction, capability, data type, allowed edge classes, and behavior.\n- **Node/graph instance:** placement and current interaction state.\n- **Collector:** a derived handle context with projected identity, side, direction, owner, and point.\n\nA Handle never replaces a Port and never supplies relationship semantics. It renders the place through which those semantics are manipulated.\n\n## Handle content and compositing\n\nHandle definition graphs may expose exactly three representation kinds: **SVG**, **HTML**, or declarative **Canvas**. Raster image, video, WebGL, and unfamiliar executable formats are outside this contract.\n\nThe resolved Handle surface must be transparent and passive. It receives no node chrome, card background, padding, or pointer ownership. Twilite composites that visual surface beneath the host node while preserving overflow around the endpoint. A separate transparent interaction target remains above the host node and owns hover, connection drag, and accessibility behavior.\n\nThe Handle definition owns content and intrinsic geometry. The use site owns size, rotation, and interaction state. Unsupported content falls back to the system Handle."
      },
